import json
from pathlib import Path
P=Path(__file__).parent
F={'3a':'sources/texts/codex/K161-3-01_transcription.tex','3b':'sources/texts/codex/K161-3-02_transcription.tex','3c':'sources/texts/codex/K161-3-03_transcription.tex','4a':'sources/texts/codex/K161-4-01_transcription.tex','4b':'sources/texts/codex/K161-4-02_transcription.tex'}
claims=[]; items={}
def c(group,title,file,scans,lines,quote,statement,status,proof,fate,refs,cl='A',confidence='high'):
    iid=f'SA23-{700+group}'; cid=f'C-SA23-spaces-{len(claims)+1:03d}'
    loc=f'{F[file]}:{lines}'; cote='161-'+file[0]
    if iid not in items:
        items[iid]={'id':iid,'sources':[],'kind':'claim and self-flagged step','cote':cote,'scans':scans,'lines':loc,'his_words':quote,'statement':title,'reconstruction':'Modern qualifications and counterexamples are marked in the associated claims; no transcription is changed.','check':'agrees','check_reason':'Direct full reading of the source; Codex lead notes carry no indexed verdict rows.'}
    else:
        items[iid]['lines'] += '; '+loc
        items[iid]['scans'] = sorted(set(items[iid]['scans']+scans))
        if cote not in items[iid]['cote']: items[iid]['cote'] += ';'+cote
    claims.append({'id':cid,'items':[iid],'cote':cote,'scans':scans,'lines':loc,'his_statement':quote,'statement':statement,'status_on_pages':status,'computation':proof,'class':cl,'fate':fate,'fate_date':'SGA 4 seminar 1963–1964, volume I published 1972; EGA II 1961 and EGA IV 1964–1967 where cited. Manuscript leaves here are undated: relative priority is not established. Modern references are dated individually in references.bib; no first-circulation date is inferred from a later edition.','present_status':'As of 19 November 2026: '+fate,'refs':refs.split(';'),'confidence':confidence})

c(1,'Bifaisceaux and products of topoi','3a',[4], '1-52','Π(E,F)','For Grothendieck topoi E=Sh(C,J), F=Sh(D,K), bifunctors C^op×D^op→Set which are sheaves in each variable present E×Top F. Equivalently use F-valued sheaves on C. Choose small sites in fixed universes.','Construction and proof sketched.','Factor cover topologies generate J∨K on C×D; their sheaf categories intersect. The source Hom(φ(x),y) test has the wrong variance; see readings.','Established product-site construction; Lafforgue 2024 slide 47 states the same arbitrary-small-site theorem. This verifies the theorem, not every intermediate formula.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','B')
c(2,'Product morphisms','3a',[5,6],'53-183','π(u,v)^*(φ)(X,Y)=?','For geometric morphisms u:E→E′ and v:F→F′, the product direct image on bifaisceaux is B↦[(X′,Y′)↦B(u*X′,v*Y′)]. For a fixed site C the induced direct image is objectwise v_*, with inverse image the sheafification of objectwise v*.','Direct image supplied; inverse-image right side blank.','Adjunction of composition with v* and v_* follows objectwise before sheafification. A direct image is a right adjoint; source calls v* the right adjoint, which must be flagged.','The missing inverse image is the left adjoint, computed by the product-site associated-sheaf construction. A naive unsheafified v* need not preserve all sheaf limits.','spaces_Lafforgue2024;spaces_SGA4I','A')
c(1,'Finite product universal property','3a',[7],'184-225','w(c,d)=u(c)×v(d)','If C,D have finite limits, Lex(C×D,X)≃Lex(C,X)×Lex(D,X), where the functor associated to (u,v) is u(c)×v(d). This gives the 2-product of their presheaf topoi.','Proved on page after the scan 6 question.','Every (c,d)=(c,1)×(1,d); a jointly left-exact functor is forced to have that form. Finite limits commute with finite limits. Separately left-exact is a different condition.','The universal property is established; source proof checked, with the terminal-object decomposition doing the work.','spaces_SGA4I;spaces_Lafforgue2024')
c(3,'Arbitrary small products','3a',[8,9],'226-310','produits de topos','For a small family of finite-limit sites C_i use the finite-support product category (terminal in all but finitely many coordinates). Sheafify the presheaf topos for all factor-cover topologies. This produces the small 2-product of the topoi.','Proof sketched, culminating in the corollary.','Finite-support decompositions reduce each lex functor to a family of lex functors; the join of the factor topologies imposes all factors simultaneously.','Established product construction. Universe bounds and finite support matter; the ordinary unrestricted category product is not the displayed construction.','spaces_SGA4I;spaces_Lafforgue2024')
c(4,'Cancelled descent sketch for limits','3a',[9],'311-349','diagramme de descente','A crossed-out diagram suggests forming more general pseudolimits through descent or Čech data. It does not specify enough coherences or a complete indexing hypothesis to certify that particular construction.','Entire passage cancelled.','No numerical computation. The missing coherence data are mathematically substantive.','General bilimit results for Grothendieck topoi do not identify the cancelled diagram uniquely. Retain it as an identifiable direction, not a completed theorem.','spaces_SGA4I','F','medium')
c(5,'Products of topological spaces versus topoi','3a',[10],'350-393','π=π′','There is a geometric embedding Sh(X×Y)→Sh(X)×Top Sh(Y), induced by the inclusion of the separate-cover topology in the usual rectangle-cover topology. It is an equivalence exactly when the corresponding locales agree, equivalently when the localic product has enough points.','Proposition and comparison stated.','The rectangle functor is not injective on objects because all empty rectangles coincide; use a basis functor or identify empty rectangles. Source first labels the separate-cover site by Top(X×Y), before distinguishing π′.','Established localic comparison; Lafforgue slide 48 states the canonical map and sufficient local compactness condition. The embedding and spatialization description follow from the two topologies.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','B')
c(6,'Compact-neighborhood proof','3a',[10,11],'387-433','voisinages quasi-compacts','If one factor has a basis of quasi-compact neighborhoods, the two product topologies agree. Quasi-compact neighborhoods need not themselves be open.','Proof supplied.','For x choose quasi-compact T with x∈int(T)⊂U. A rectangular cover of T×{y} has a finite subcover; intersect the corresponding neighborhoods of y. This proves the needed frame inequality locally, then join over x,y.','The standard locally compact sufficient condition is established. The proof uses quasi-compactness, not Hausdorffness, and is checked directly.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','B')
c(7,'Locally closed compact-point refinement','3a',[11,12],'434-480','Raffinement','The same comparison holds if every nonempty locally closed subspace of one factor has a point with a basis of quasi-compact neighborhoods in that subspace, in the relative sense used in the proof.','Refinement proved by maximal-open argument.','Join all opens U′ for which the required frame inequality holds. In a nonempty locally closed complement choose a relatively compact neighborhood, use finite rectangular subcovers, and enlarge U′, a contradiction. Check that the relative neighborhood condition is available inside every chosen open.','The argument supports this sufficient condition. Searches found the ordinary local-compactness theorem, not an exact independently named theorem for this formulation; no novelty or unresolved problem is inferred.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','C','medium')
c(8,'Infinite products with compact tails','3a',[12,13],'481-549','tous sauf un','The infinite product comparison is an equivalence under the displayed local quasi-compactness and quasi-compactness assumptions. The intermediate cylindrical-site comparison needs only all but finitely many factors quasi-compact; a compact tail gives finite control.','Proof sketched over two leaves.','Finite products are handled by the preceding theorem; a compact product tail gives a finite subcover in the cylinder argument. Standard classical choice is understood. Several W/U and supremum-index slips are recorded without emendation.','A compact-tail sufficient result is justified by the written argument. The broader necessary-and-sufficient classification is not supplied.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','C','medium')
c(9,'Unfinished infinite discrete counterexamples','3a',[13,15],'550-576','Contr’exemples pour des produits infinis','The leaves seek failure of spatiality for infinitely many discrete factors. The apparent blanket criterion “not almost all finite” is not valid if read as covering every such family: N^N is a counterinstance.','Unfinished cover test, ending “ou encore”.','For countably many countable discrete factors, the locale has countably many cylinder generators and relations, hence is spatial by Heckmann. Direct prefix-tree proof is given in proof_checks. The written basic neighborhood fixes cofinite rather than finite coordinates.','Heckmann gives spatiality of countably presented locales (online 10 November 2014; journal October 2015). This settles the countable test negatively for the blanket assertion; it does not rule out other nonspatial products.','spaces_Heckmann2015','C')
c(10,'Limits of essential topoi','3a',[16],'577-621','Il est douteux','A filtered inverse limit of presheaf topoi with essential transition morphisms need not be a presheaf topos. The additional site-adjoint hypotheses in the leaf are a stronger construction and must be kept separate.','Doubt followed by a conditional construction; projection inverse image left blank.','Counterexample: Cantor space is the inverse limit of finite binary discrete spaces. Its sheaf topos is the corresponding coherent localic limit; it has no nonempty supercompact opens and cannot be a localic presheaf topos. Under the source stronger hypotheses, inverse image is a left Kan extension along the colimit-site functor, subject to the stated flatness.','General negative answer via the Cantor example; conditional presheaf-site description remains valid with the extra hypotheses. No historical reason for the author’s doubt is recorded.','spaces_SGA4I;spaces_Lafforgue2024','C','medium')
c(11,'Density and extension from a site','3a',[18,19],'622-782','β(u₀)(F)=lim u₀(X)','For a small site C and cocomplete target E, left Kan extension along ε:C→Sh(C) is βu₀(F)=colim_(C/F)u₀(X). A sheaf is the corresponding colimit of sheafified representables.','Construction and adjunction announced and developed.','The category-of-elements cocone proves the Kan universal property. If ε is fully faithful, C/εX has terminal object X, hence the unit u₀→αβu₀ is an isomorphism.','Standard density/Kan extension; SGA I and II supply the presheaf density and associated-sheaf framework. Subcanonicity alone does not imply βu₀ preserves colimits.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(12,'Restriction–extension adjunction','3a',[19,20],'768-871','données d’adjonction','β is left adjoint to restriction α. Restriction of cocontinuous functors Sh(C)→E is fully faithful. Its image consists of u₀ whose Kan extension is cocontinuous and whose unit is invertible.','Adjunction sketched; cocontinuity explicitly doubted.','Use density to identify the counit on cocontinuous functors. The printed β codomain Hom(C,E) is wrong: it is Hom(Sh(C),E).','The adjunction and conditional image characterization are standard. The question about automatic cocontinuity has a negative answer; see the cover test below.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(13,'Localization and cocontinuity','3a',[21],'872-935','OK','Cocontinuous functors out of Sh(C) correspond to cocontinuous functors out of PSh(C) that invert associated-sheaf equivalences.','Question answered “OK” on the same leaf.','If a:PSh(C)→Sh(C), colim_Sh=a(colim_PSh). An equivalence-inverting cocontinuous functor factors through a and preserves the induced colimits.','Established reflective-localization universal property. This is a completed page argument, not an open question.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(14,'Cover epimorphisms do not suffice','3a',[21],'936-945','Cela est-il suffisant ? non','Sending covering families to jointly epimorphic families is necessary but not sufficient for a functor C→E to extend cocontinuously to Sh(C). The attempted continuation at 161-3 scan 22 remains unfinished.','Question with marginal “non”; attempted proof breaks off.','On the discrete two-point space let u(∅)=∅ and u(U)=1 for every nonempty open. Every cover maps to a jointly surjective family, but the disjoint cover of the whole space gives 1⊔1→1. Thus it cannot be the restriction of a coproduct-preserving functor Set²→Set.','Negative answer established by this explicit finite counterexample. The correct condition is inversion of sheafification equivalences, or the appropriate colimit descent relations.','spaces_SGA4I','B')
c(15,'Alexandrov topology–preorder adjunction','3b',[24,25],'11-167','β ⊣ α','For the convention y≤x iff x∈closure{y}, β assigns lower-set topology to a preorder and α the specialization preorder. β is fully faithful and left adjoint to α. Its image is the spaces with a smallest open neighborhood at each point, equivalently arbitrary intersections of opens are open.','Proof and tasks; neighborhood proof supplied.','Continuous βI→X iff monotone I→αX. αβ=id and T⊂βαT. β preserves finite limits but not infinite products; countably many two-point discrete spaces give a non-Alexandrov product. Inequality in line 83 reverses the preceding convention.','Classical Alexandrov-space correspondence; precise order convention checked directly. Antisymmetry corresponds to T0, called “présobre” on the leaf rather than in standard modern terminology.','spaces_SGA4I','E')
c(16,'Irreducibles and pro-objects of a preorder','3b',[26],'169-216','Interpréter Pro(X)','For an Alexandrov lower-set space, nonempty irreducible closed sets are upward closed and downward directed. They describe the points of its presheaf topos and the sobrification; their point order is opposite inclusion under the leaf’s convention.','Several exercises stated, not fully proved.','A closed set is upward closed; irreducibility says any two open intersections meet, equivalently directedness. Nonemptiness is required and cannot be dropped. Minimal-neighborhood basis covers are trivial, giving Sh(X)≃PSh(X).','Established via the presheaf point/flat-functor description and SGA IV 4.2, 7.1, 7.5. The pro-object orientation is tracked explicitly.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(17,'Sober Alexandrov spaces and descending chains','3b',[27],'218-238','Prouver que','For a poset in classical set theory, every nonempty upward-closed downward-directed subset has a least element iff there is no infinite strictly descending sequence. This is the sobriety criterion for its Alexandrov space; the related sobrification-essentiality assertion uses the same principal-filter condition.','Exercise and equivalence list.','If a directed upper set has no least element, recursively construct a strict descending sequence. Conversely the upper closure of a strict descending chain is directed and has no least element. Dependent choice is used in the recursive direction.','Criterion verified directly; the category-of-points interpretation is standard. The text’s δ phrase has an ambiguous X versus its sobrification, retained in readings.','spaces_SGA4I','B','medium')
c(18,'When a presheaf topos is spatial','3b',[27],'226-256','Ĉ localique ssi C préordre','PSh(C) is localic iff C is a preorder. In that case it is Sh(C) for the Alexandrov topology. A sober space has a presheaf sheaf-topos iff its points possessing minimal open neighborhoods are sufficiently dense to give a basis of such neighborhoods.','Stated as exercises and equivalences; revisited and proved at scan 49.','Subterminal generators force each representable’s elements to have unique comparison arrows; equivalently each Hom_C is at most singleton. Conversely representables in a preorder are subterminal and form generators. “Very dense” must mean the basis condition, not merely ordinary density.','Established localic-presheaf classification, with the density qualification explicit. Scan 49 physical 235–247 repeats the proof and essential-point criterion.','spaces_SGA4I','B')
c(19,'Morphisms in the preorder equivalence','3b',[27],'247-256','2-catégorie ... ensembles préordonnés','The proposed equivalence with preorders is correct for essential geometric morphisms, but not for all geometric morphisms between these topoi without modifying the morphisms on the preorder side.','Unqualified proposed 2-equivalence; recovery question.','The Alexandrov poset Z has a nonprincipal directed upper set Z, hence an extra geometric point of Sh(Z) not represented by any element of Z. A functor from the one-point preorder can choose only a principal point.','The unrestricted morphism claim needs correction. SGA IV 7.6 explicitly separates essential morphisms from arbitrary geometric morphisms.','spaces_SGA4I','C')
c(20,'Frame extension from rectangles','3b',[29],'258-283,346-383','commute aux Inf finis ?','Given frame maps α:O(X)→O, β:O(Y)→O, define γ(W)=∨_(U×V⊂W)α(U)∧β(V). It preserves finite meets and its rectangle values have the expected factor form. Arbitrary joins, and hence universality as a frame map, require the product-locale comparison condition.','Finite-meet calculation supplied; bijectivity and last rectangle formula queried.','Distribute finite meets across joins and intersect rectangles. To recover α(U) use rectangles U×Y; handle empty rectangles separately. Arbitrary covers are exactly the missing condition, not a consequence of the finite-meet computation.','The finite calculation is valid; full bijectivity is conditional and equivalent to the earlier product question in the spatial setting.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','C')
c(21,'Trigonometric and Gram calculations','3b',[29],'284-345','a=cos θ','For three unit real vectors with common pairwise inner product t=cosθ, Gram positivity requires −1/2≤t≤1. The displayed coordinate solution has b=cotθ(1−cosθ), c²=(1−t)(1+2t)/(1+t), for sinθ≠0. Also cos3θ=4cos³θ−3cosθ.','Mixed live and cancelled calculation.','compute_checks.py: symbolic simplification, Gram determinant/eigenvalue factorization and triple-angle identity. At θ=0 handle directly; θ=π is impossible. Cancelled ||u+v+w||²=3+6cos3θ conflicts with common inner product cosθ and should be 3+6cosθ.','Elementary identities settled by exact calculation. Products uv in the crossed passage are not defined sufficiently to identify an additional quaternion assertion.','spaces_Elementary','E')
c(22,'Morita cancellation with compatible data','3b',[31,33],'385-458,529-601','Lemme ... équivalence','For a locally ringed topos and positive finite-rank locally free modules, (G,E)↦(End(G),E,G⊗E,canonical isomorphism) is an equivalence onto quadruples (A,E,F,φ:A⊗End(E)≅End(F)), with compatible morphisms.','Gerbe/local automorphism proof sketched and completed.','Reconstruct G=Hom_End(E)(E,F). Evaluation G⊗E→F is an isomorphism locally by matrix units; the A action gives A≅End(G). Positive rank makes E faithful. Matrix centralizer check is recorded.','Standard Morita equivalence establishes the precise quadruple statement. The source local-inner argument works after replacing “automorphism of F” by the indicated algebra automorphism of End(F).','spaces_Morita','B')
c(22,'Uniqueness qualification in the Morita corollary','3b',[33],'593-601','unique à isomorphisme unique','G is unique up to unique compatible isomorphism only when the given identification G⊗E≅F is retained. An assertion merely A≅End(G) has scalar automorphisms and line-bundle twists. Zero rank must be excluded.','Corollary compresses the compatibility data.','At a point with G=k, all k× automorphisms induce identity on End(G), disproving unrigidified unique-isomorphism language. With E=F=0 the algebra A is unconstrained, disproving a zero-rank extension.','Established corrected formulation; no additional open cancellation problem remains after the data are specified.','spaces_Morita','C')
c(23,'Torsors, complexes and hypercohomology fragment','3b',[32],'460-527','TORS^n ... H^n','The diagram relates torsor-like groupoids to cohomology of complexes, and writes a chain-homotopy relation f−f′=dk±kd. Degree conventions and the higher torsor objects are not specified.','Fragmentary diagram; question-like marks not recoverable as a precise verbal problem.','For cochain maps with degree −1 homotopy the conventional formula is dh+hd. H¹ classifies torsors for a sheaf of groups; higher nonabelian hypercohomology requires additional structure.','Classical H¹ and abelian-complex specializations are known, but they do not identify the entire n-indexed diagram. Keep the unidentified part class F rather than claiming a higher-gerbe theorem is written here.','spaces_SGA4I','F','medium')
c(24,'Sheaf operations and internal algebra','3b',[35],'603-638','Recall','Inverse images preserve finite limits and colimits; sheaf constructions for algebraic structures are defined internally. Statements about all topology or all kinds of structure are lecture programme, not unrestricted equivalences of invariants.','Lecture outline with examples.','Global sections are Hom(1,F), not Hom(F,1). Arbitrary products of epimorphisms may fail to be epi. For non-geometric first-order structures inverse image preservation is not automatic.','The categorical facts are established; the programme’s scope qualifiers are retained. The global-sections formula needs a reading alarm.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(25,'Products of epimorphisms of sheaves','3b',[35],'614-619','produit d’épimorphismes','Countable products of epimorphisms need not be epimorphisms in a sheaf topos.','Example requested but not supplied in this excerpt.','On R let F_n be the coproduct of subterminal sheaves U_n=(−1/n,1/n) and V=R minus {0}. Each F_n→1 is epi. A product section near 0 would choose U_n on a common neighborhood for every n, impossible; hence ∏F_n→1 is not epi.','Explicit standard counterexample verifies the warning, including surjectivity of each factor and failure at the stalk at 0.','spaces_SGA4I','B')
c(26,'Sobrification and recovery of spaces','3b',[35,37],'640-685,755-802','bijective is a little more subtle','sob(X) consists of irreducible closed subsets, with opens defined by intersection with opens of X. X→sob(X), x↦closure{x}, induces an isomorphism of frames and an equivalence of sheaf topoi. Maps Sh(X)→Sh(Y) correspond to continuous X→Y when Y is sober, with specialization 2-cells.','Sketch with reference to SGA/Hakim and practical-scope caveats.','The open-set correspondence proves the sheaf equivalence; the universal property gives a left adjoint to inclusion Sob→Top. Its colimit preservation is in Sob, not necessarily in Top. The displayed Hom adjunction has reversed arguments.','SGA IV 4.2.1–4.2.4 and 7.1.6 give the exact results. No claim that wild-space singular homotopy is reconstructed is imported.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(27,'Specialization and stalk maps','3b',[39],'875-917','x∈closure{y}','If x lies in closure{y}, every neighborhood of x contains y, inducing F_x→F_y. For sheaves on a space, natural transformations between stalk functors form the corresponding specialization preorder; sobriety identifies all topos points.','Calculation and claimed equivalences.','Subterminal sheaves determine necessity and uniqueness. The prose reverses x/y in its neighborhood explanation, and this leaf switches the earlier preorder convention.','SGA IV 4.2.3 and 7.1.6 establish the point category statement, with the orientation tracked rather than silently standardized.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(28,'Giraud axioms and lexical localizations','3b',[39],'918-935','What will insure ... ?','A Grothendieck topos is a left-exact reflective localization of a presheaf category on a small category; equivalently it satisfies Giraud exactness and small-generator axioms.','Motivating question followed by a definition; detailed axioms referenced elsewhere.','Smallness and existence/exactness conditions cannot be omitted. A generic list of attractive exactness properties without a generator is not sufficient.','SGA IV Theorem 1.2 supplies the exact axiomatic equivalence. The question is answered at the foundational level, not left as a new open problem.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(29,'Scope of classifying topoi','3b',[37],'767-802','possibly all','Classifying topoi classify models of geometric theories in Grothendieck topoi. The parenthetical suggestion “possibly all” kinds of structure cannot mean arbitrary first-order theories with arbitrary inverse-image stability.','Qualified lecture assertion.','Geometric formulas use finite conjunction, arbitrary disjunction, and existential quantification. Products and inverse images provide direct tests showing why unrestricted limit-theory or first-order generalizations need distinct hypotheses.','Established geometric-theory framework; exact unrestricted claim is not supported. Lawvere’s algebraic theories address the finite-product special case, not all geometric theories.','spaces_Lawvere1963;spaces_Lafforgue2024','C')
c(30,'Species of structures and 2-representability','3b',[36,38],'687-753,804-873','Supposons qu’il existe','A structure assignment T(C) equipped with underlying objects and functoriality may be represented as Lex(β,C) or by the specified limit shapes. If representation exists, 2-Yoneda gives uniqueness up to equivalence and pseudonatural transformations T→F correspond to F(β).','Definitions followed by an explicit existence hypothesis.','The 2-Yoneda implication is formal. It is not a proof that every proposed assignment T has a representing category. Strict versus pseudo functoriality and universes are separate data.','Lawvere’s finite-product theory and later finite-limit/accessible-category frameworks establish specified cases. The page’s conditional statement is valid; universal existence is not asserted there.','spaces_Lawvere1963','B')
c(31,'Fields are not a finite-product algebraic theory','3b',[40],'937-969','il n’existe pas','The full category of nonzero commutative fields with its underlying-set functor cannot be the models of a finite-product algebraic theory extending commutative rings in the proposed way.','Investigation with the beginning of an argument.','Models of a product theory are closed under products computed on underlying sets. F₂×F₂ is not a field. This also obstructs a finite-limit theory with the same full underlying-ring interpretation.','Negative answer already follows from Lawvere semantics. Geometric axioms for fields are possible but are a different kind of theory.','spaces_Lawvere1963','B')
c(31,'Field-valued tests for ring maps','3b',[40],'953-969','Hom(B,k)→Hom(A,k)','A ring map A→B inducing bijections on maps into every field corresponds on spectra to a bijection on points with isomorphisms of residue fields; this alone does not mean A→B is an isomorphism. The class of such maps is closed under colimits of arrows.','Last part of the live calculation.','Test against each residue field for the pointwise criterion; maps into a field forget nilpotents. Hom(−,k) turns colimits into limits, preserving objectwise bijections.','The pointwise criterion is standard. Interpret the page’s “u bijective” as its spectral map, not the ring homomorphism. Nilpotent thickenings give immediate non-isomorphisms.','spaces_StacksSpec','E')
c(32,'Cancelled field-recognition test','3c',[42],'34-49','Cette application est bijective ssi C est un corps','The cancelled map Z[t]→Z[t,t⁻¹]×Z, t↦(t,0), tests whether every element has a unique reflexive generalized inverse. In commutative rings this is von Neumann regularity, not the field property.','Whole rotated draft cancelled; attempted connectedness argument unfinished.','Z[x,y]/(x(xy−1),y(xy−1))≃Z[t,t⁻¹]×Z by Chinese remainders. F₂×F₃ passes the test but is not a field. Exact finite enumeration compares it with Z/4.','The literal field conclusion is false; the correct regular-ring interpretation is verified directly. Cancellation must remain visible; no claim of an author-endorsed final theorem.','spaces_StacksRegular','C')
c(33,'Recovering a theory from its set models','3c',[44,46,48],'90-192,216-232','en fonction de T(Ens) ?','Under full-embedding/2-cartesian compatibility and objectwise presheaf-model assumptions, T(C) is recovered from S=T(Set) and underlying functors β_i as the S-valued presheaves whose β_i-components are representable. The action on λ-functors, existence of free objects, and equivalence with Hom_λ(R,C) remain separate questions.','Explicit hypotheses; necessary conditions supplied; cocompletion passage cancelled.','Yoneda gives the object reconstruction. Representability preservation along a general λ-functor is not automatic from merely listing S and β_i; a small arity category and suitable accessibility/colimit hypotheses are required.','Lawvere settles finitary algebraic theories using finite free models and finite products. The broader arbitrary-λ reconstruction is only partially specified here; no exact unconditional theorem is claimed.','spaces_Lawvere1963','C','medium')
c(34,'Generators, density and representability','3c',[41],'3-8','C ... generating','A small full generating subcategory of a Grothendieck topos is dense for its induced canonical topology; its restricted Yoneda embedding is fully faithful and has a left-exact left adjoint. Representability of limit-preserving functors additionally uses solution-set/size hypotheses.','Compressed lecture remarks.','“Any subcategory” must include the relevant fullness/density hypotheses. Generating does not make an arbitrary nonfull subcategory dense. Smallness is necessary in the adjoint/representability argument.','SGA I §7 and IV 1.2 give the precise setting; the broad representability sentence is conditional rather than a universal theorem for all categories.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(35,'Classifying group topoi and their points','3c',[41,43],'10-31,51-58','H^i(B_G, )=H^i(G, )','For a discrete group G, B_G is the topos of G-sets, its cohomology is group cohomology, and the forgetful fiber functor has automorphism group G with the appropriate opposite convention. More generally points of PSh(C) are flat functors, equivalently Ind(C^op) as fiber functors.','Examples and asserted equivalence.','The group-cohomology comparison uses derived invariants. The category of points is the opposite of the fiber-functor category, giving Pro(C) in the geometric convention. A G-equivariant sheaf point over x has stabilizer automorphisms.','SGA IV 7.2 and 7.5 establish the classifying/presheaf cases. The leaf’s spatiality question is merged with scans 27 and 49.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(36,'Topoi as a 2-category','3c',[43],'60-80','2-catégorie','Geometric morphisms and transformations form a 2-category; equivalence is an inverse up to invertible 2-cells. For localic targets, morphisms correspond to frame maps from opens into subterminal objects.','Lecture exposition.','The direct-image and inverse-image conventions reverse the direction of 2-cells; keep the source convention. Equivalence of underlying categories with geometric structure yields equivalence of topoi.','Established in SGA IV §§3–4; the warning against early passage to isomorphism classes is substantive.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(37,'Sierpiński classification and embeddings','3c',[43,45],'82-124','Tout espace top. de préordre se plonge','The Sierpiński topos classifies a subterminal object. Every T0 space embeds in a product of Sierpiński spaces using its opens. An arbitrary non-T0 preorder space does not topologically embed in such a product.','Classification asserted, followed by an overbroad embedding sentence.','Two indistinguishable distinct points cannot embed in a T0 product. The opens-evaluation map embeds the T0 quotient. If Sub_E(1)≅O(X), the localic reflection E→Sh(X) is universal for maps into spatial localic topoi.','Established classification and localic reflection; the embedding statement needs T0 or quotient qualification.','spaces_SGA4I','C')
c(38,'Presheaf morphisms and Kan adjoints','3c',[45,50],'128-153,249-271','u_! u* u_*','For u:C→D, restriction u*:PSh(D)→PSh(C) has left and right Kan adjoints and defines an essential geometric morphism. If C,D have finite limits and u is lex, u_! is lex and is itself an inverse-image functor in the opposite geometric direction.','Live exposition plus wholly cancelled adjoint computation on scan 50.','Yoneda shows u_!y(c)=y(u(c)). If u has right adjoint g and g has right adjoint h, restrictions/right Kan extensions on representables match the written adjunction chain; preserve each hypothesis.','SGA I §5 and IV 7.6 give the exact essential-morphism framework. The cancelled calculation is mathematically identifiable but not silently reinstated as live text.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(38,'Geometric maps out of a presheaf topos','3c',[45],'143-153','Hom_top(Ĉ,E)≃Hom(C,Pt(E))','A geometric morphism PSh(C)→E amounts to a functor C→Pt(E), with the page’s point convention. If E=PSh(D), this is a functor C→Pro(D). Conversely maps E→PSh(C), with C finite-limit, are lex functors C→E with reversed transformation convention.','Equivalences stated.','Evaluate the inverse image E→Set^(C^op) at each c: finite-limit and colimit preservation are pointwise. Taking the opposite fiber-functor category gives the displayed variance.','Standard presheaf universal property, matching SGA IV 7.5–7.6. A functor C→D gives only the representable/essential subclass on the target-point side.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(39,'Slices, terminal and empty topoi','3c',[47],'194-214','u_! u* u_*','The slice E/X→E has inverse image Y↦Y×X, left adjoint forgetting the map to X, and right adjoint dependent product. Maps E/X→E/Y over E form the discrete category Hom_E(X,Y). Set is terminal; the empty topos is the one-object category and admits a map into it only from a degenerate topos.','Examples; right-adjoint formula left “more complicated”; sums heading undeveloped.','The dependent product is characterized by Hom_E(T,Π_XA)=Hom_E/X(T×X,A). The discreteness and classification follow from the slice universal property.','SGA IV localization formalism establishes these facts. The absent explicit formula is recovered by the adjunction, not treated as an unresolved question.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(40,'Subgroup localization','3c',[51],'274-303','(B_G)_{/G/H}≃B_H','For a subgroup H⊂G, G-sets over G/H are equivalent to H-sets by taking the fiber over eH; inverse is G×^H−. Restriction has induction and coinduction adjoints.','Construction and covering-space interpretation supplied.','The coset eH is a section of the underlying object, not generally a G-fixed global section in B_G. For connected locally path-connected semilocally simply connected spaces, covering-space classification identifies subgroups and connected pointed coverings.','Established subgroup/induction theorem. The source’s local-connected/local-simply-connected hypotheses are sufficient when interpreted in the usual pathwise covering setting.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(41,'Equivariant sheaf localization','3c',[52],'304-314','Top(Z,G)_{/Z′}→Top(Z,H)','For a discrete group G acting on Z and H⊂G, restriction to H identifies H-equivariant sheaves with G-equivariant sheaves over Z×G/H with diagonal action.','Equivalence stated with an ellipsis.','Fiber over the eH section gives the functor; induction reconstructs the sheaf. The displayed (X,e) in the fiber product should have the base-space type (Z,e).','Established equivariant slice equivalence, a sheafwise version of the preceding subgroup calculation.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(42,'Sites and generated topologies','3c',[53,55],'315-345,370-379','Cov 1 ... Cov 3','Pullback, composition and identity define a pretopology; generated sieves give its topology. Topologies have intersections and joins. Sheaf/separated conditions can be tested after pullback on generating covering families. The canonical topology is maximal subcanonical.','Exposition with formulas.','Hom(R,F) is the limit over the category of elements of R, not C/F. Čech equalizers require products in both appropriate terms; omissions in displays are reading alarms. Saturation is distinguished from pretopology axioms.','SGA II §§1–3 establishes the constructions. The mathematical content is standard, while several short formulas need typographic checking.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(43,'Sheafification and recovery of topology','3c',[55],'381-417','i∘a≃L∘L','The plus construction LF(X)=colim_(R∈J(X))Hom(R,F) is lex, makes F separated, and makes separated F into a sheaf. L² is sheafification, left adjoint to inclusion. Limits of sheaves are computed as presheaves; colimits are sheafified. A sieve covers iff its sheafification is the representable’s sheafification.','Theorems and corollaries stated; final sentence incomplete.','Filteredness of covering-sieve refinements and commutation of filtered colimits with finite limits prove lexness. Density gives F=colim_(C/F)a(X). The epimorphic-family criterion recovers J.','SGA II §3 and IV §1 give these established results. The terminal unfinished sentence is recoverable only to the extent of the explicit preceding criterion.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(44,'Comparison lemma and subtopoi','3c',[54],'346-368','Comparison lemma','A full subsite with induced topology and covering every object yields an equivalent sheaf topos. Grothendieck topologies on C correspond order-reversingly to strictly full lex reflective subcategories of PSh(C). A small full generating subcategory with canonical induced topology presents its topos.','Theorems stated.','Use covering descent to recover every sheaf from restriction; the lex reflector recovers covering sieves by which monomorphisms become isomorphisms. The page contains opposing finer/less-fine words, recorded in readings.','SGA III comparison lemma and IV 1.2 establish the claims. The advertised converse requires the stated subcanonical/small-site hypotheses.','spaces_SGA4I')
c(45,'Universe bounds for sheafification','3c',[55],'383-400','fix universe ... U-sites','The plus construction and categories of generators must be formulated with a fixed universe and small/cofinal covering data; one may treat a topos as a site without pretending its objects form a small set.','Explicit task in margin.','Size is handled by U-sites and a small generating subsite; arbitrary large colimits are not silently used.','SGA I §0 and II size conventions provide the solution. The page leaves the exposition to be filled in, not an unresolved set-theoretic theorem.','spaces_SGA4I')

c(46,'Faithful flatness and fibers','4a',[3],'5-36','B fid. plat sss B plat et Spec B→Spec A surjectif','For a flat A-module M, faithfulness is equivalent to M⊗κ(m)≠0 for every maximal ideal m. For an A-algebra B, faithful flatness is equivalent to flatness plus surjectivity of Spec(B)→Spec(A); it implies A→B and A/I→B/IB injective.','Main proof plus unfinished marginal sufficiency proof.','Take a nonzero cyclic submodule A/I⊂N; flatness preserves its injection and nonvanishing is detected at a maximal ideal containing I. The main proof has =0 where ≠0 is required. The margin omits the flatness hypothesis on M.','EGA IV and Stacks Algebra faithful-flatness criteria establish the result. The fiber test alone does not imply flatness: a nonflat module can have nonzero fibers everywhere.','spaces_StacksFlat','A')
c(47,'Flat local maps and openness','4a',[3,4],'38-48','f plat ... points maximaux','A flat local homomorphism of nonzero local rings is faithfully flat. A flat scheme map is generizing and maps generic points of irreducible components to generic points. A flat locally finitely presented map is universally open.','Statements and corollary.','Apply the local faithful-flatness criterion to the stalks; primes generalizing the image lift. “Point maximal” here must mean maximal under generization, not a closed point: A¹→Spec k[t] localization gives the distinction.','Established flatness/openness theorem; finite presentation is essential for openness in general.','spaces_StacksFlat;spaces_StacksOpen','A')
c(48,'Flat base change of closures','4a',[4],'50-92','f⁻¹(closure T)=closure f⁻¹(T)','For flat f:S′→S and T the image of a quasi-compact morphism Z→S, inverse image commutes with closure. Faithfully flat quasi-compact maps are submersive, universally after base change.','Algebraic proof and alternative generization proof sketched.','On affines closure of image is V(ker(A→C)); flatness preserves the kernel after base change. Quasi-compactness permits reduction using finitely many affine pieces. A saturated closed subset upstairs descends by the closure equality.','Stacks Morphisms 29.26.12, 29.26.16 and fpqc descent give the exact hypotheses. The quasi-compact-image assumption must not be discarded.','spaces_StacksClosure;spaces_StacksDescent','A')
c(49,'Descent of topological subsets','4a',[4],'94-116','LF ... ?','For fpqc covers, arbitrary subsets descend as sets; open and closed subsets descend topologically. Locally closed subsets descend with the quasi-compact/retrocompact qualifications needed for the closure argument.','Exactness sequences; a locally closed row is explicitly questioned.','The map |S′×_S S′|→|S′|×_|S| |S′| is onto via nonzero tensor products of residue fields. Quotient topology then handles open and closed subsets. The arbitrary locally closed assertion fails in the immersion example below.','The open/closed statements are established. The questioned extra finiteness condition is substantive, not merely a typographical uncertainty.','spaces_StacksDescent;spaces_StacksImmersion','C')
c(50,'Descent of finiteness and morphism properties','4a',[5],'119-184','surj, radiciel, plat ...','Surjectivity and universal injectivity descend under surjective base change. Quasi-compactness and quasi-separatedness descend under quasi-compact surjective base change. Finite type/presentation, flatness, separation, properness, affineness, integrality, finiteness, quasi-affineness, quasi-finiteness, smoothness, and étaleness descend fpqc locally on the target with their standard hypotheses.','Proof outline and extended checklist.','Algebra finite generation/presentation descends along faithful flatness by choosing finitely many coefficients and testing the remaining cokernel/kernel after tensoring. Diagonal properties reduce quasi-separatedness/separation. The page interchanges f and g in the statement at line 127.','Stacks Descent §35.23 gives separate exact lemmas; this record preserves the individual hypotheses instead of treating the entire checklist as one automatic theorem.','spaces_StacksDescent','A')
c(51,'Formal smoothness in the descent checklist','4a',[5],'176-182','formellement lisse (?)','The page asks whether formal smoothness descends fpqc locally on the target. Formal étaleness is listed separately; these are infinitesimal lifting properties and not equivalent to smoothness without local finite presentation.','Question mark in checklist.','One must distinguish target descent from locality on the source and distinguish formal lifting from finite presentation. No finite computation can settle this.','Stacks Algebra lemma-descent-formally-smooth proves faithful-flat base descent without finite presentation, using descent of arbitrary projectivity. The standard smoothness equivalence adds local finite presentation.','spaces_StacksFormal','B')
c(52,'Effective descent of quasi-coherent modules','4a',[6],'190-228','descente effective ... Modules qu. coh.','An fpqc cover is universally effective descent for quasi-coherent modules and algebras. Compatible submodules of a given descended module descend; finite generation, finite presentation, and finite local freeness descend.','Theorem; “explain what this means” is a pedagogical task.','The affine descent object is the equalizer of the two descent maps; the cocycle identity and faithful flatness show base change recovers the given module. Preserve the actual cocycle datum, not only equality of isomorphism classes.','Stacks Descent §§35.3,35.5 and Algebra descent of finite projectives establish the result.','spaces_StacksQC;spaces_StacksDescent','A')
c(53,'Descent of scheme morphisms','4a',[4,6],'50-52,230-244','Hom_S(X,Y)→Hom(X′,Y′)⇒Hom(X″,Y″)','Representable functors are fpqc sheaves: compatible morphisms between already descended schemes descend uniquely. Consequently a morphism becoming an isomorphism after fpqc base change was an isomorphism.','Corollaries with reduction to affines.','Use affine descent of ring maps after covering the source and target; the equalizer condition is essential. This is full faithfulness, not effectiveness for arbitrary scheme descent data.','Stacks 35.13.7 and §35.35 prove the exact assertions. Arbitrary schemes need not form an effective stack for every fpqc descent datum.','spaces_StacksMorphDescent','A')
c(54,'Arbitrary immersion descent fails','4a',[5,6],'174-178,228,246-248','imm. ... arbitraire','Closed and open immersions descend fpqc locally; quasi-compact immersions do too. The live corollary extending this to arbitrary immersions is false without an additional hypothesis.','Question first cancelled; later arbitrary immersion asserted.','Let X=⊔_p Spec F_p→Spec Z. Its image is not locally closed, so it is not an immersion. B=∏_p Z_(p) is torsion-free, hence flat over Z, with B/pB=F_p for every p, hence faithfully flat. After base change, X embeds closed into the open ⊔_p Spec Z_(p)⊂Spec B. Every hypothesis is checked in proof_checks.','Positive result: Stacks 35.23.23, tag 02L8. Arbitrary immersions are fppf-local, §35.24. The explicit counterexample is posted there by Vihaan Dheer on 28 August 2026, comment 11722; this run verifies its proof independently and does not call it the historical first counterexample.','spaces_StacksImmersion;spaces_StacksDescent','C')
c(55,'Affine descent and its consequences','4a',[6,7],'250-272','descente eff. ... schémas relatifs affines','Fpqc descent is effective for affine schemes via quasi-coherent algebras. Affineness, quasi-affineness, integral, finite, and finite locally free morphisms descend.','Theorem and corollaries.','Relative Spec converts algebra descent contravariantly. Integrality reduces elementwise to finite A[x]-modules; finiteness is module descent. Quasi-affineness uses the canonical map to Spec(f_*O).','Established fpqc descent; Stacks §35.23 and §35.37 provide the individual results.','spaces_StacksAffine;spaces_StacksDescent','A')
c(56,'Descent of a specified ample line bundle','4a',[7],'274-292','L (très) ample ...','Given an invertible sheaf L already defined on X, relative ampleness descends under fpqc base change on the base. Very ampleness likewise descends in the finite-type/quasi-compact setup where the evaluation map into P(f_*L) is available.','Proof by evaluation and flat base change; broader ample criterion sketched.','For qcqs f, flat base change identifies f_*L after pullback. Surjectivity of evaluation and immersion of its classifying map descend with the needed quasi-compactness. Do not confuse descent of L with existence of some ample L upstairs.','Stacks Descent 35.25.4 proves descent of relative ampleness. The source’s very ample proof must retain the finiteness hypotheses ensuring its projective embedding argument.','spaces_StacksAmple','A')
c(57,'Projectivity and effectiveness caveats','4a',[7],'294-296','les seuls exemples ... projectifs et quasi-projectifs','Projectivity or quasi-projectivity need not descend merely because some relatively ample bundle exists after an fpqc cover; compatible descent of a particular polarization is stronger. The phrase “only examples” is not a universal classification of all possible morphism properties.','Remark and bare heading about effectiveness.','A descended ample line bundle yields the positive theorem; an arbitrary upstairs ample bundle need not carry descent data. The non-quasi-compact immersion counterexample gives another failure for the literal “only” formulation.','Modern counterexamples and polarized descent distinguish these issues. Stacks example on projective descent and §35.36 explicitly record non-effectivity for general projective descent data. No reason for the author dropping the heading is documented.','spaces_StacksProjective','C')
c(58,'Radicial maps and universal homeomorphisms','4a',[8],'299-330','On peut prouver la réciproque','A scheme map is radicial iff universally injective, iff injective on all field-valued points, iff its diagonal is surjective, iff each fiber has at most one point with purely inseparable residue extension. Integral, surjective, radicial maps are exactly universal homeomorphisms. A locally finitely presented generizing map is open.','Equivalences stated; converse universal-homeomorphism theorem deferred.','Residue-field tensor products test universal injectivity. Integral maps are universally closed, so bijectivity yields the forward universal homeomorphism. The converse requires the integrality theorem and is not proved by set theory alone.','Stacks Morphisms §§29.10,29.45 and open-morphism results give the precise theorems. Cusp normalization and purely inseparable field extensions are valid examples with their standard finite/integral hypotheses.','spaces_StacksRadicial;spaces_StacksOpen','A')
c(59,'Quasi-affine criteria and Zariski main theorem','4a',[9],'333-365','Conditions équivalentes','For a quasi-compact quasi-separated scheme, quasi-affineness is equivalent to O_X ample, to the canonical map into Spec Γ(X,O_X) being an open immersion, and to global generation of every quasi-coherent module. The associated principal-open basis tests require their exact scheme hypotheses. A separated quasi-finite map is quasi-affine.','Equivalence list and theorem.','The canonical affinization and ample O_X give standard equivalences. Separated quasi-finite maps factor as an open immersion followed by a finite map; their quasi-compactness yields quasi-affineness. A merely topological-embedding criterion is retained only in this qcqs canonical-map setting.','EGA II ampleness criteria and Zariski main theorem establish the statements; Stacks quasi-affine and quasi-finite sections give exact modern formulations.','spaces_StacksQuasiAffine;spaces_StacksZMT','A')
c(60,'Proper, finite and projective distinctions','4a',[9],'367-385','projectif = fermé + quasi-projectif','Finite morphisms are exactly proper morphisms with finite fibers; proper plus quasi-projective gives projective under the page’s projective-space convention. Replacing proper by merely closed is false.','Implication diagram and two displayed characterizations.','A¹_k→Spec k is closed and quasi-projective but is not proper or projective. Its base change A¹×A¹→A¹ maps the closed hyperbola xy=1 onto D(x), which is not closed. This checks failure of universal closedness explicitly.','The finite/proper/quasi-finite theorem is established. The printed “fermé” must be checked against ink; universal closedness would repair that implication, but no repair is made here.','spaces_StacksFinite;spaces_StacksProjective','C')
c(61,'Ampleness tests and tensor products','4a',[10],'388-440','EGA II 4.5','For qcqs X, ampleness of L is characterized by principal opens of positive-power sections forming an affine basis, or eventual global generation of finite-type quasi-coherent modules. Over an affine base and for X finite type, positive powers become very ample. For composed morphisms with the displayed quasi-compact assumptions, L⊗f*M^n becomes relatively ample for n large.','Criteria and tensor-product proposition; implication sign doubtful.','The finite-type hypothesis is used for uniform very ample powers. The formula c′ prints n₀ where n varies; tensor-ampleness also needs the relevant quasi-compactness to make the exponent uniform.','EGA II §4.5 is explicitly cited by the manuscript. Original Theorem4.5.2 and Proposition4.5.5 give the equivalences;4.5.6–4.5.8 give tensor/power consequences;4.6.11 and4.6.13(ii) give finite-type very-ampleness and composed-morphism tensor ampleness, with the exact quasi-compactness assumptions. These primary loci were directly checked.','spaces_EGAII;spaces_StacksAmple','A')
c(62,'Diagonal–section–equalizer calculus','4a',[11,12],'444-547,559-585','vérifier sur diagonal, sections, noyaux','For a class M containing isomorphisms and stable under base change and composition, graph factorization proves gf∈M and Δ_g∈M⇒f∈M. Membership of Δ_g is equivalent to the corresponding condition for all base-changed sections/equalizers/graphs.','Exercise and diagrams; earlier version cancelled.','Factor f as Γ_f followed by projection, with Γ_f a base change of Δ_g. Necessity of the universal cancellation property follows by taking the diagonal/section special case. The assumptions “contains isomorphisms” and all base changes are required.','Standard categorical diagram lemma; direct proof checked. It organizes, but does not automatically prove, each listed scheme-property example.','spaces_StacksMorphisms','E')
c(63,'Separatedness and equality principles','4a',[11,12],'483-494,583-585','principe d’identité ... réduits','Separatedness means the diagonal is closed; equalizers of maps into a separated target are closed. Two morphisms from a reduced source agreeing on a dense open agree everywhere, under the usual density and separatedness hypotheses. Intersections of affine opens in a separated scheme are affine.','Definitions and examples; compressed “identity principle” phrase.','The equalizer is a closed subscheme; if it contains a dense open in a reduced source, its ideal vanishes. Density only on underlying points without reducedness does not eliminate nilpotents.','Established in the diagonal criteria and scheme separatedness theory. The statement is a qualified identity principle, not equality from arbitrary pointwise evidence.','spaces_StacksMorphisms','A')
c(64,'Quasi-separatedness, coherence and direct images','4a',[11,12],'496-523,596-629','cohérents = qu. cpt qu. sép','Quasi-separated means quasi-compact diagonal; equivalently intersections of quasi-compact opens are quasi-compact when the ambient hypotheses are fixed. “Coherent morphism” on these pages means qcqs. For qcqs f and quasi-coherent F, f_*F and R^if_*F are quasi-coherent, and flat base change holds. Locally noetherian schemes are quasi-separated.','Definitions and theorem checklist.','The qcqs assumptions allow finite affine Čech arguments locally on the base. The margin gf finite type, g quasi-separated⇒f “quasi-separated” likely intended finite type; retain as reading alarm. A unique closed point plus quasi-compactness characterizes local schemes in the stated scheme setting.','Established scheme-cohomology results. “Coherent” here must not be confused with coherent modules or a noetherian hypothesis.','spaces_StacksQCoh','A')
c(65,'Relative Spec and projective bundles','4a',[13],'632-670','P(M) ... V(M)* / G_m','For a quasi-coherent module M, P(M)=Proj Sym(M) represents invertible quotients of pullbacks of M. The open of surjective linear forms V(M)* is a G_m-torsor over P(M), and the Zariski sheaf quotient is P(M). Relative Spec represents quasi-coherent algebra maps.','Representability programme and assertions.','On the standard section charts the line quotient is trivial and V(M)*→P(M) has a section. These charts prove the torsor/quotient statement; do not equate the punctured total space with a naive deletion of a zero section for arbitrary non-locally-free M.','EGA II projective spectrum and Stacks Constructions projective-bundle/relative-Spec sections establish the functors, including arbitrary quasi-coherent M with the stated quotient interpretation.','spaces_StacksProj;spaces_StacksRelativeSpec','A')
c(66,'Morphisms to an affine scheme','4a',[15],'672-702','Hom(X,Spec A)≃Hom(A,Γ(X,O_X))','For a locally ringed space X, morphisms X→Spec A correspond to unital ring homomorphisms A→Γ(X,O_X). The point x maps to the kernel of A→κ(x), and principal opens pull back to invertibility loci.','Complete constructive proof sketched.','Invertibility of a germ is open. Localizations A_f map into sections on the inverse image of D(f); these maps glue and yield local stalk maps. The construction is inverse to global sections.','Established affine-scheme adjunction, Stacks Schemes 26.6.4. This also identifies k-schemes with schemes over Spec k.','spaces_StacksAffineAdj','A')
c(67,'Zariski sheaves and representability by open gluing','4a',[15,17],'704-729,753-789','Comment exprimer ... faisceau ?','A functor on affine schemes is a Zariski sheaf iff it satisfies the finite principal-open equalizer condition, including the empty cover. Such a sheaf is represented by a scheme iff it admits a covering by representable open subfunctors; affine opens suffice.','Question followed by representability criterion and applications.','For rings and f_i generating the unit ideal use F(A)→∏F(A_fi)⇉∏F(A_fifj). Glue affine representatives along their open intersections; the sheaf condition identifies the resulting functor. Coproducts are disjoint and universal; affine relative schemes have all small limits.','Established functorial construction of schemes. At line 774 “to be a sheaf iff it is a sheaf and ...” must read as a representability question mathematically, but remains unchanged in the source.','spaces_StacksRepresentable','A')
c(1,'Product-topos fragment and false membership test','4b',[18],'1-79','p_*(F)∈Ĉ, q_*(F)∈D̃','The product comparison Fun(C^op,E)≃PSh(C)×Top E is true, but membership in a product subtopos cannot in general be tested merely by its two direct images belonging to the factors.','Comparison questioned, embedding argued, final essential-image identification unfinished.','Take C=D=[0→1], with point subtopoi evaluated at 0, whose direct images are constant-arrow diagrams. Set F00=2 and F01=F10=F11=1; all maps toward F00 select 0. Projection direct images evaluate the other coordinate at terminal 1, hence both are constant 1; F is not constant and is outside the product point subtopos. Exact computation saved. The initially considered discrete matrix was rejected because a point direct image uses terminal, not empty, components. The related cancelled fragment is 161-4 scan 16, lines 731–750.','Lafforgue slide 47 verifies the product theorem. The final proposed criterion is false and cannot be used to complete this page’s proof.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','C')
c(68,'Finite disjoint sums and constant group schemes','4b',[20,21],'84-154','sommes finies ... disjointes et universelles','Finite coproducts of affine schemes are spectra of finite product rings; they are disjoint and universal. The finite-set functor I↦⊔_I Spec k preserves finite limits and makes finite groups into constant affine group schemes, with coordinate ring k^I and comultiplication induced by multiplication.','Proof by idempotents and tensors.','A_i⊗_(∏A_j)A_j=0 for i≠j; idempotents decompose every base-changed algebra. k^I is the function algebra, not the group algebra k[I] in its modern usual notation.','Classical affine algebra verified directly; finite I is essential to the stated geometric coproduct formula.','spaces_StacksSpec','E')
c(69,'Point sets of fiber products','4b',[21,22],'157-263','fibre ... Spec κ(x)⊗κ(s)κ(y)','The underlying-point map |X×_S Y|→|X|×_|S| |Y| is surjective, with fiber Spec(κ(x)⊗_κ(s)κ(y)); it need not be injective. If one morphism is a monomorphism, the corresponding point-set square is cartesian. Closed/open immersions and localizations give the stated induced topology.','Statements and requested example.','Tensor products of nonzero field extensions over a field are nonzero. Example Spec C×_Spec R Spec C has two points because C⊗_R C≃C×C, while each factor has one. Over algebraically closed k, generic points of A¹×A¹ still produce multiple fibers.','Established scheme fiber-product theorem. Finite-type k-rational/maximal points over algebraically closed k preserve finite limits as sets, but the Zariski topology is not the product topology.','spaces_StacksFiber','A')
c(70,'Closure of an affine image','4b',[21],'173-184','formule pour closure φ(Z)','For u:A→B and J⊂B, the closure of the image of V(J) in Spec A is V(u⁻¹J). Dominance is equivalent to ker(u)⊂Nil(A).','Formula requested; dominance criterion written.','Apply the standard closure-of-affine-image formula to A→B/J. Radicalizing the inverse image does not change V. The source Ker φ means kernel of the ring map, not the topological map.','Classical affine-spectrum calculation; proof supplied here and matched to scheme-theoretic-image references.','spaces_StacksClosure','E')
c(71,'Noetherian-space criterion and components','4b',[22],'189-194','espace noethérien = ...','A space is noetherian if every closed subset has finitely many irreducible components and descending chains of irreducible closed subsets stabilize. In particular a locally finite family of irreducible components controls connected components by finite incidence.','Criterion and topic fragment.','A descending chain of closed sets produces a finitely branching tree of proper irreducible descendants; an infinite strict chain gives an infinite branch, contradicting the irreducible-chain hypothesis. The converse is immediate. Quasi-compactness stated separately is redundant under the full criterion.','Standard noetherian topology; the connected-component sentence does not specify a further theorem beyond the locally finite union argument.','spaces_StacksTopology','E')
c(72,'Affine schemes as equation functors','4b',[23,24],'268-356','V_J(k′)≃Hom(A_J,k′)','For arbitrary variable sets I and equations generating J⊂k[T_i], the solution functor on all k-algebras is represented by k[T_i]/J. Every affine scheme arises this way. Closed subfunctors correspond to ideals; arbitrary monomorphisms correspond to ring epimorphisms, which need not be surjective.','Lecture development with Yoneda invoked.','Evaluate in the universal algebra to recover the ideal exactly. Z→Q is a ring epimorphism but not surjective, showing why monomorphism and closed immersion differ. Finite generation corresponds to finitely many coordinates; finite presentation also needs finitely many relations.','Classical affine Yoneda theory, with Hilbert basis theorem when the base is noetherian. The phrase “algebras over A” should match the base k in context.','spaces_StacksAffineAdj;spaces_StacksSpec','E')
c(73,'Field points forget nilpotents','4b',[25,26],'358-413','Importance ... éléments nilpotents','Restriction of an affine equation functor to field-valued points determines only the radical of its ideal. Nil(A) is the intersection of all prime ideals, equivalently kernels of maps to fields. Functions on V_A are morphisms to A¹ and recover A only when the full functor is retained.','Proposition and examples.','k[ε]/(ε²) and k have the same field-valued points, but evaluating the full functor on dual numbers distinguishes them. For finite-type algebras over algebraically closed fields, rational-point tests use the Nullstellensatz. Over a general base, finite extensions of all prime residue fields also suffice: pass to a fiber and choose a closed point after localizing at the element to detect.','Classical Nullstellensatz and nilradical results. The general-base statement is valid when residue fields means all prime residue fields; restricting only to closed residue points would require a Jacobson-type qualification.','spaces_StacksNullstellensatz','E')
c(74,'Cancelled compact-topos fragment','4b',[26],'414-450','Topos associé à l’espace compact I^E','The cancelled diagram invokes slice topoi, internal Hom, a two-element or infinite set, and a compact power I^E, but no complete assertion can be recovered.','Entire working block cancelled.','No defined map or quantifiers permit a sound computation. A compact-power interpretation alone would not identify the intended claim.','Not identifiable from the supplied text. Keep it separate from the established product results.','spaces_Lafforgue2024','F','low')
c(75,'Affine limits and algebraic group kernels','4b',[27,28],'451-539','C=?','All small limits of affine k-schemes correspond to colimits of k-algebras. Fiber products use tensor products; equalizers use the quotient by all u(a)−v(a); filtered inverse limits use filtered direct limits of rings. Kernels and stabilizers of affine group actions are the indicated fiber products.','Construction and examples; lower square left C=?.','The square’s missing algebra is B⊗_(A⊗A)A≃B/(u(a)−v(a)). This gives SL_n=ker(det), and simultaneous stabilizers are intersections or the stabilizer on a product. Code checks the polynomial equalizer example.','Classical affine-category calculations; the missing C is determined by the universal property and does not remain an open problem.','spaces_StacksFiber','E')
c(76,'Affine quotients may lose geometry','4b',[28,29],'541-594','G/B dans Aff_k est e_k','Colimits in affine schemes are opposite limits of rings, but can differ from sheaf or scheme quotients. For GL_n and its upper-triangular subgroup, the flag-variety quotient has only constant global functions over a field, so its affine quotient is Spec k.','Warning and example; finite-relation quotient topic deferred.','The flag variety is proper geometrically connected with Γ(O)=k; invariant regular functions on GL_n descend along its torsor to the flag variety and are constant. Finite relations require exact effectiveness hypotheses and cannot be certified from “OK” alone.','The GL_n/B example is established. The general finite-equivalence-relation quotient assertion is left qualified; finite locally free relations have standard effective affine quotients, while arbitrary finite relation statements need further analysis.','spaces_StacksQuotient','C')
c(77,'Coproduct points and idempotent decompositions','4b',[28,29],'550-592','n’est pas bijective ... k′ connexe','For a finite family of rings A_i, maps ∏A_i→R correspond to orthogonal idempotents e_i summing to 1 and maps A_i→e_iR. The naive disjoint union of point sets is bijective for connected nonzero R, but not general R. The empty affine scheme is strict initial.','Computation with a questioned punctuation mark; continued next leaf.','For R=F₂×F₂ and A₁=A₂=F₂ there are four maps F₂²→R but only two maps choosing a single component. For R=0 all points coincide; hence the source’s injectivity condition R≠0 matters. Infinite products admit nonprincipal ultrafilter points and are excluded.','Classical idempotent decomposition verified exactly. Retain finiteness from the preceding discussion rather than extending the formula to arbitrary I.','spaces_StacksSpec','E')
c(78,'Spectrum topology and radicals','4b',[30,31],'613-681','V(∩J_i)=V(∏J_i)=∪V(J_i)','Spectrum closed sets are V(J), determined by radicals. Arbitrary sums give intersections; finite products/intersections give unions. Specialization reverses prime inclusion. Spec A is quasi-compact and sober; irreducibility of reduced nonzero A is equivalent to A being a domain.','Detailed proof and corollaries.','A finite principal-open subcover follows from an expression 1=Σa_if_i. Irreducible closed subsets are V(p) with unique generic point p. A sum of radical ideals need not be radical: (y)+(y−x²)=(y,x²) in k[x,y]. Exact ideal computation recorded.','Classical spectral facts and nilradical calculations. Source line 620 says primes containing A where J is required; line 643 field-valued point notation has a restricted k that should be checked.','spaces_StacksSpec','E')
c(79,'Localization and the structure sheaf','4b',[31,32],'683-778','Le préfaisceau précédent est un faisceau','D(f)⊂D(g) iff f∈√(g), giving M_g→M_f. Localized modules on principal opens satisfy the sheaf equalizer condition and glue to a quasi-coherent sheaf with stalk M_p; for M=A the stalk is the local ring A_p.','Construction; sheaf proof reduced to f=1 then omitted.','Clear denominators finitely, use powers of covering generators still generating the unit ideal, and glue matching localized sections by a partition-of-unity expression. Uniqueness follows from the same finite-power argument. M_f=M⊗A_f by localization’s universal property. Margin A_f→A_g reverses the required map.','Standard affine quasi-coherent sheaf theorem. The missing proof is supplied in proof_checks; no scans are used to correct the written arrow.','spaces_StacksStructureSheaf','A')

